22:06:10 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’ve put your finger on a subtle but crucial point: if older food is *always* more broken down and newer food is *always* chunkier, then a system that sorts by chunkiness would indeed produce exactly the same output order as a time-based queue. The deli line and the blender would look identical in practice, even if the internal mechanism differs. The problem, however, is that the relationship between “age” and “chunkiness” is far from perfect — it’s full of exceptions that break the queue illusion. The stomach isn’t just indirectly sorting by age; it’s sorting by a physical property that often, but not always, correlates with time. And when those two things diverge, the stomach betrays its true nature. Let me give you a few concrete counterexamples that make this crystal clear. --- ### 1. Liquids and Beverages: The Newcomer Jumps the Line Entirely Imagine you eat a large, solid meal — say a chicken breast with fibrous vegetables — and then, 10 minutes later, you drink a glass of water or a clear juice. What happens? The stomach doesn’t hold the water until the solids are done. The water rapidly distributes and begins emptying almost immediately, taking advantage of gastric sieving: liquid is already the perfect size. The pyloric sphincter lets it flow. The solid meal is still churning away for an hour or more. In a true time-ordered system, the water (the “new customer”) would have to wait behind the chicken. In reality, the water exits first. That’s a direct violation of first-in-first-out. ### 2. Chewing Differences: Older Food Can Be Chunkier Than Newer Food This one is critical. Suppose you take a first bite of an apple and chew it thoroughly — it enters the stomach as a fine, mushy paste. Then you take a second bite and barely chew it, swallowing a large, hard chunk. Now the stomach has: - **Bite 1:** old, but small, soft, and nearly liquefied. - **Bite 2:** younger, but large and solid. If the stomach truly sorted by age, Bite 1 would move on first and Bite 2 would wait its turn. But if Bite 2 is too large to pass the pylorus, it will be retained and ground for a much longer time than the well-chewed first bite. In fact, the first, older bite will be emptied while the newer, unchewed chunk is still being churned. The order of arrival is irrelevant; only the physical state matters. The stomach has no way to “know” that the large chunk came second. It just sees a big particle and holds it back. ### 3. Gastric Sieving Can Push Older Material *Backward*, Mixing It with Newer Food The stomach’s muscular waves don’t simply move everything in a forward direction. In the antrum, strong contractions squeeze the chyme toward the pylorus, but the sphincter only allows small particles and liquids through. The larger pieces — including some that are older but still not broken down enough — are forcefully pushed back into the body of the stomach. This retropulsion effectively re-mixes older, partially broken bits with the fresher contents. So an older, semi-solid particle that was near the exit might be shot backwards, ending up sitting next to newly swallowed food. A time-based system would never shove an earlier customer to the back of the line; the stomach does this regularly. ### 4. Indigestible Objects: Oldest Item Waits the Longest, Regardless of Age If you swallow a marble (unintentionally) with your breakfast, it will stay in the stomach until a specific phase of gastric motor activity — called the migrating motor complex, during a fasted state — sweeps it out. This could take many hours, long after all other digestible food from that meal (and even subsequent meals) has left. The marble is older than everything you eat later in the day, yet it exits last. That’s because the stomach’s selection criterion is size and density, not time. ### So, Is Your Original Point Basically Correct? If by “basically correct” you mean that, under typical conditions with uniformly chewed, solid meals, older food tends to leave before newer food because it’s had more time to break down, then yes, there’s a correlation that makes the queue analogy *seem* plausible. But that correlation is an emergent property, not a rule or a mechanism. The stomach achieves that outcome without ever registering the age of a food particle. It’s like a sieve that separates pebbles from sand; if you continuously tumble a mixture, the sand (originally pebbles that have been worn down) will fall through first. You could say the sieve “sorts by how long they’ve been tumbling,” but that’s just a by-product of a system that only cares about grain size. The distinction matters because in the stomach, as I’ve shown, the time-size link breaks in many common and important scenarios. **Your health teacher’s error wasn’t just a simplification; it was stating a mechanism that doesn’t exist.** The stomach doesn’t “process food in the order you eat it.” It processes food in a continuous, chaotic mix where exit is granted based solely on physical readiness. That readiness is influenced by time, but also by chewing, food composition, liquid content, and the stomach’s own mixing behavior. So, no, your original point — that the stomach differentiates between “newer” and “older” food in a way akin to a deli line — isn’t correct, because the stomach never tracks which is which. It’s not a subtle, indirect sorting by age; it’s a direct sorting by physical condition, and the two diverge as soon as you look closely.
